  "question": "If $F(x)$ is an antiderivative of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ with $F(0)=0$, find $F(\\frac{1}{2})$.",
  "answeroption": "B",
  "explanation": "We know $\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}dx=\\sin^{-1}x+C$.<br /><br />Given $F(0)=0$, so $0=\\sin^{-1}(0)+C$ giving $C=0$.<br /><br />Thus $F(x)=\\sin^{-1}x$.<br /><br />Therefore $F(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sin^{-1}(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$.",
